course: dictionary
lesson: 4369
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. «Hi, guys!» is said to a group of friends, so guys means people. «He's a nice guy» means a man, so guy means a man in that sentence. Always look at the words around the word first.
explain: Guy can also name a thing: «Pitch the tent and tie the guy to the peg.» Here a guy is a rope that keeps the tent steady, because the words tent and peg are the clues. Ask: what would fit here? Then try your meaning in the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: Some uses depend on where you live. In the UK, a guy is a figure of a man burnt on a bonfire on Bonfire Night, so the clue is the bonfire. In the USA, Canada and Australia, people often say «come on, guys» in casual speech, which means relaxed, friendly talk. Priya's «bad guy» is a phrase, and it means something that causes trouble.
fact: Guy is a noun. Its plural is guys.
fact: «Guy» (informal) means a man, as in «he's a nice guy».
fact: «Guys» (informal) means people in a group, men and women, as in «Hi, guys!».
fact: «Guy rope» means a rope that holds a tent or pole steady.
fact: In the UK, a guy is a figure of a man burnt on a bonfire on Bonfire Night.
exercise: «Hi, guys!» Who is the word guys used for here? | One old man | *A group of men and women | A figure burnt on a bonfire | A tent rope
exercise: «He's a nice guy.» What does guy mean in this sentence? | A rope for a tent | *A man | A group of friends | A bonfire figure
exercise: «Pitch the tent and tie the guy to the peg.» What is a guy here? | A stick for the tent | A figure of a man burnt on a bonfire | *A rope that holds the tent steady | A man who lives in the tent
test: In the UK, what is a guy on Bonfire Night? | A type of firework | A rope for a tent | *A figure of a man burnt on a bonfire | A friend who lives next door
test: Priya said the timer was the «bad guy». What does bad guy suggest? | A group of friends | A tent rope | *Something that causes trouble | A man who is always kind
exam: Which sentence uses guy to mean people in a group? | "He's a nice guy." | "Tie the guy to the peg." | *"Come on, guys, let's go!" | "The guy was burnt on Bonfire Night."
